{"sequence":31,"timestamp":"2026-03-29T22:10:39.451128794Z","event_type":"amendment_queued","details":{"amendment_id":"fr-2-adf10e30","body":"Align the shipped default process preset with the new named stoichiometric constant. `ProcessParams::default()` now uses `NITRIFICATION_ALK_MEQ_PER_MG_N` in `crates/tank_core/src/types/process.rs:337-338`, but the runtime default preset loaded from `crates/tank_data/data/process/default.toml:52-54` still hard-codes `0.1428`, and scenario startup converts that preset into live params in `crates/tank_scenarios/src/lib.rs:346-349`. As written, two code paths that both claim to use the default process parameters will consume different alkalinity and can diverge in deterministic comparisons.","dedup_key":"42ba737d4f920ebfb686f2712ef128e710a93daf0cb562650c45bc7fee0f5a23","run_id":"run-20260329204233","source":"workflow_stage","source_stage":"final_review"}}
{"sequence":32,"timestamp":"2026-03-29T22:10:39.459585751Z","event_type":"amendment_queued","details":{"amendment_id":"fr-2-abc5b76f","body":"Fix alkalinity assertion messages to use `meq` consistently. `crates/tank_core/src/budget_helpers.rs:471-476` and `crates/tank_core/src/budget_helpers.rs:494-507` still hard-code `mg` in the per-system and per-stage lines, so a failing `Element::Alkalinity` assertion prints the wrong unit even though the headline/before-after lines were updated to `meq`.","dedup_key":"7a16f547727a03bd23af5a0ebf8751209ded1011d7598b3a91a135784fc7555c","run_id":"run-20260329204233","source":"workflow_stage","source_stage":"final_review"}}
